#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129017 :question/id #uuid "059deff9-dfa3-5c3e-88b1-194af41d3ed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7347" :question/problem "![](images/17/1782385c55493892be0308ede1e4fbe5308ea9440e21b70cc2da7d7f01fb8dcd.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362 :topic/math-academy-id 271 :topic/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Sine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703 :topic/math-academy-id 831 :topic/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049321 :topic/math-academy-id 4135 :topic/title "Polar Coordinate Transformations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488 :topic/math-academy-id 207 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94118c9f-f252-5126-95dc-39997a9303de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{5}r^{3}\\cos θ\\sin θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126573} {:answer/id #uuid "a19a57f2-5551-5378-ae40-8fe5a86f9afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}r^{3}\\cos θ\\sin θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126574} {:answer/id #uuid "bb19ef63-baf8-585f-b58d-ebdee10fd1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{5}r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126575} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d69333-7a0c-54e6-81f2-56b5cc028849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126576} {:answer/id #uuid "4efd860c-2bdc-522c-a4aa-77ae001cab6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126577}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5d71c0-1eda-5098-a773-eb6dafd40de1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126572}] :question/id #uuid "96701d26-3867-5d84-962d-70db90473eae" :question/math-a